    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0594" n="494" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg494"/>

                <head>No. 321.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Sir Edward
                    Thornton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-10-28"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 28,
                        1879</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 22d instant, in reply to one of this Department of the 12th
                    of August last, relative to encroachments by Canadian fishermen within the
                    jurisdiction of the United States, and to express the gratification with which
                    this Department has learned of the prompt attention given to the subject by the
                    Canadian Government. It is hoped that the proposed action of the Canadian
                    authorities will supersede the necessity of any seizures or forfeitures on the
                    part of the United States.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
